# Interface configuration template - MULE
#
# Configures interface bring-up and addressing for every bearer and for the
# mesh interface batman-adv presents.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Every value below is TBD. This file is an input to
# configuration generation, not a file to be edited on a node. The final format
# depends on the network management stack, which is TBD; this file describes
# what must be configured, not yet how.
#
# Trades: TBR-NET-01 (field address prefix and allocation scheme)
#         TBR-RF-01, TBR-RF-03 (which physical interfaces exist)
#         TBR-LINUX-01 (interface naming and driver bring-up behaviour)
#
# ADDRESSING IS NOT DECIDED
#
# TBR-NET-01 is open. Address family, whether the prefix is fixed across all
# deployments or set per deployment in the mission package, and whether host
# addresses derive from node identity or are allocated, are all open.
#
# One consequence worth flagging here: an address derived from a durable node
# identifier IS a durable identifier, visible to anyone observing traffic. See
# THREAT_MODEL.md.
#
# The scheme must not collide when two independently built deployments meet at
# an incident. That is the specific failure TBR-NET-01 exists to prevent, and
# it becomes more likely the more successful this program is.

# --- Interface inventory ----------------------------------------------------
# Names are TBD and depend on the selected hardware block and on driver
# behaviour. Do not hardcode a kernel-assigned name; use a stable naming rule.
#
#   Logical function              Interface   Addressing        Trade
#   ---------------------------   ---------   ---------------   -----------
#   Sub-GHz HaLow mesh member     TBD         none, member of   TBR-RF-02
#                                             the batman mesh
#   High-rate inter-node bearer   TBD         TBD               TBR-RF-01
#   EUD access point              TBD         TBD               TBR-RF-03
#   batman-adv mesh interface     TBD         TBD               TBR-NET-01
#   LoRa plane                    TBD         not IP            n/a
#
# Note: interfaces that are members of the batman-adv mesh carry no IP address
# themselves. The mesh interface does. Configuring an address on a member
# interface is a common error that produces confusing symptoms.
#
# The LoRa plane is not an IP bearer. It carries the independent low-bandwidth
# degraded-communications plane and does not appear in IP routing at all.

# --- Bring-up ordering ------------------------------------------------------
# Ordering matters and is a known source of failure:
#
#   1. Driver and firmware loaded, radio enumerated.
#   2. Physical interface up.
#   3. Association: mesh point or access point.
#   4. batman-adv routing algorithm set (BEFORE adding interfaces).
#   5. Interfaces added to the batman-adv mesh.
#   6. Mesh interface up and addressed.
#   7. Services started.
#
# A mesh interface configured before its driver has finished initialising, or
# batman-adv attached to an interface that is not yet up, fails in ways that
# look like a radio fault. Ordering belongs to the systemd unit structure and
# is TBD, pending TBR-LINUX-01.
#
# FML-ADR-023 records that the upstream MANET reference project encodes real
# knowledge about bring-up ordering. Where its configuration is adopted, cite
# the source in a comment on the adopting line.

